---

<ChapterLayout
  title="7.0 — Cultural Age · AST 100"
  description="The last age: fire, farming, cities, industry — and a species that reconstructed the history of the Universe and began listening for anyone else who had done the same. Seven milestones, four lessons, and the end of the course."
  activeId="7.0"
  chapter={7}
  current="/chapter/7/7.0"
>
  <Hero
    tease="The final age, and the shortest by far. In the last million years — a rounding error on this course's clock — one branch of one primate lineage learned to control fire, to farm, to write, to burn buried sunlight, and finally to reconstruct the entire fourteen-billion-year story of which it is the latest sentence. Seven milestones, four lessons, and one question left open."
    client:load
  />

  <article class="topic-page">
    <div class="prose-cosmic lesson-prose">
      <h2 id="fire">Fire, Farms, and Cities</h2>
      <p>The <strong>Cultural Age</strong> — Chapter 7 of <em>Our Cosmic History</em>, and its last — covers the shortest
        span in the course and the fastest change in it. Across <strong>four lessons</strong> and <strong>seven
        milestones</strong>, it follows one African primate from the edge of a fire to the edge of the observable
        universe.</p>
      <p>It begins with <strong>the control of fire</strong> — possibly used as far back as a million years ago, but only
        an everyday tool after about 400,000. Fire is external digestion: it makes food safe and easy to eat, which pays
        for an expensive brain. Then the <strong>emergence of humans</strong> (200–300 ky), and a long, quiet stretch in
        which we are never more than a few hundred thousand strong. What used to be called a sudden <strong>"cultural
        explosion"</strong> around 50,000 years ago now looks more like the visible tail of a slow African accumulation —
        art, ornament, and burial appearing gradually across the continent.</p>
      <p>Then the curve bends. The <strong>Agricultural Revolution</strong> (10 ky) ties people to land and multiplies
        what a hectare can feed. The <strong>rise of civilisation and the state</strong> (5 ky) brings cities, writing,
        and specialists — and lets knowledge outlive the person who had it. The <strong>Industrial Revolution</strong>
        (250 years ago) gives each person the energy of dozens of servants, by burning the buried forests of the
        Carboniferous. Eight billion of us now live in the <strong>"Life Era"</strong>: the first moment in this story
        when the dominant force shaping a planet is a species' choices.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("7.1")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">7.1</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">History of the World</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="maps">Redrawing the Universe</h2>
      <p>Every age draws the universe and puts itself somewhere in the picture. We began at the exact centre of creation,
        with the Sun and stars wheeling around us — a model that worked for fourteen centuries and predicted the planets
        well. It broke on a single stubborn observation: the backwards loop of Mars. Copernicus moved the Sun to the
        middle, Kepler replaced circles with ellipses, and Thomas Digges quietly let the stars run on forever. The modern
        map is not a map of where at all, but of <em>when</em>: from the Big Bang to us. Every step demoted us, and every
        demotion turned out to be right.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("7.2")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">7.2</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">History of Mapping the Worlds</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="sky">The Sky We Live By</h2>
      <p>Long before any of that, the sky was a tool. The Sun's changing height gives the <strong>year</strong> and told
        every farming society when to plant. The Moon's phases give the <strong>month</strong> — the word is simply
        "moon", worn down. The seven wandering lights of the naked-eye sky give the <strong>names of the days</strong>,
        in English and in Bangla alike: Thursday is Jupiter's day, and so is <em>Brihaspatibar</em>. And the constellations
        are memory devices good enough to carry a canoe across the Pacific. The sky was humanity's first calendar, clock,
        compass, and library.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("7.3")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">7.3</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Role of the Sky in Culture</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <h2 id="listening">And Now We Listen</h2>
      <p>The course ends where it must. Measured as energy flowing through each gram — the same complexity ladder we
        climbed in <a href={subPath("0.2")}>§0.2</a> — a modern society runs hotter than a brain, a plant, a planet, or a
        star: the most intense thing we know of anywhere. And it is now pointing its instruments outward, listening for
        anyone else who managed the same climb. The <strong>Allen Telescope Array</strong>, this chapter's instrument,
        was the first telescope built for that job. Sixty years of listening have produced silence — but we have searched
        only a hot tub's worth of a cosmic ocean, so the silence is not yet evidence of anything.</p>
      <a
        href={subPath("7.4")}
        class="group flex items-baseline gap-3 no-underline text-white/70 hover:text-white transition-colors duration-500"
      >
        <span class="font-mono text-[12px] tracking-[0.18em] text-plasma/80">7.4</span>
        <span class="font-serif group-hover:text-plasma transition-colors duration-500" style="font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2vw, 1.55rem);">Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence</span>
        <span class="inline-block transition-transform duration-500 group-hover:translate-x-1 text-plasma/70">→</span>
      </a>

      <hr />

      <blockquote><p>Hydrogen from the Big Bang, carbon from a dying star, gold from a collision of neutron stars — and
        an arrangement of those atoms that can look up, measure the light, and reconstruct its own entire history. The
        Universe, in one small pocket, has become able to describe itself. Whether it has managed that anywhere else is
        the one question this course leaves open.</p></blockquote>
    </div>
  </article>

